ke  as an example  a system where students can enroll in  courses and cancel their enrollment  again and so on so  typical university-based course  management system and so  an event in the log in in such a system  might be something like  student x cancel the enrollment in  course y for reason z  that's it so it's a very nice compact  uh descriptive like description  of what happened the of the event that  occurred  whereas if you look at the replication  log that a primary backup system would  produce  as a result of the equivalent change was  well you might have a bunch of tables  and maybe one table contains the  enrollments and so the cancellation of  the enrollment corresponds to deleting  a row from the enrollment table maybe  you have a separate reason to record the  cancellation reasons so we would add a  row to that table  maybe you also have for example the  the number of available places as a  denormalized field as essentially a  cache  on the course table and so then if a  student  cancels their enrollment that means you  also have to increment the number of  available places  so if you look at the log event in the  top  section here you can see that you can  reconstruct from it the fact that it  must have been  a an enrollment a cancellation of an  enrollment but it's quite an indirect  there's a lot of irrelevant detail there  about the current database schema and  how it happens to be implemented whereas  the log event in the state machine  replication approach is actually a very  nice  beautiful descriptive self-contained  uh event that's um that you know is a  lot easier to interpret  as a human working with the system  so that is one example one one reason  why you might want to use state machine  replication because these descriptive  events are actually very nice to work  with  another reason why you might want to use  state machine replication  is that the fact that you have derived  the replica state  from the events in the log through some  d